fpworld.patch - the world for a camera that stands in it (chase, shoulder, first person): the
changes to files other work owns.  docs/issues/native-first-person-world.md says what is wrong
from the ground, what each part does about it, and what was run.

Made against tools/recomp as it stood at the time in the line "snapshot" below (the camera
views, the look controls, the scene tap and the integrator's merges of that evening in it).
Apply from tools/recomp:

    cd tools/recomp && patch -p1 --dry-run < render/fpworld.patch && patch -p1 < render/fpworld.patch

The new files it needs are already in place and are not in the patch (nothing compiles them
until the two build.py are patched):
    app/fpworld.h  app/fpworld.cpp          the game side: switches, hooks, the level's fill at a level load
    render/worldfill.h  render/worldfill.cpp generated geometry (ceilings, skirts) from a level's terrain: mesh lists
    render/worldfill_main.cpp               the same from a .world file, without the game (figures, a Wavefront file)
    render/worldfill_measure.py             measurements of a level's geometry (no part of a build)

Notes for the merge
 - hle.txt gains 3 addresses (frustum_testBox, world_draw, world_drawChunk).  funcs.h changes
   with them: every translation is made and compiled again at its next build (about 5 minutes
   on a busy machine).  A program that does not link app/fpworld.cpp gets the weak
   pass-through: the game as before.
 - app/build.py and render/build.py: render/worldfill.cpp joins the renderer's core files;
   app/build.py compiles app/fpworld.cpp after fpview.cpp (with -I render: it includes
   worldfill.h).  app/build_win.py and tools/godot/build.py are NOT touched: to give those
   programs the fill, add render/worldfill.cpp to their renderer files; app/fpworld.cpp needs
   the hle.txt addresses and fpview.
 - render/vif1.h, vif1.cpp: NOP notes NUM 0xB0 / 0xB1 go to Gs::note (0xA5..0xA7 are the
   layout's, 0xA8 / 0xA9 the scene tap's); Vif1::no_reject starts entries 0x16 and 0x02
   without their clip flag while the GS model cuts triangles itself.  With no note in the
   stream nothing changes.
 - render/gs.h, gs.cpp: Gs::WorldView, Gs::world_run (strips of the four mesh programs:
   positions read as the floats the program left, cut at the near plane, texel coordinates
   given in perspective), Gs::draw_fill (the level's fill into the list).  Both are reached
   only through the notes; without them the list is the bytes it was (the tour's overhead
   pictures are the same files with and without the patch's parts switched on).
 - THE SCENE TAP AND THE CUT: render/scene_tap checks that a packet's list grew by three
   vertices per drawn triangle; a strip that world_run cut does not.  A host that installs the
   tap (Godot) does not need the cut - it draws the packets' complete geometry - and should
   run with world.clip and world.persp off (fpworld::setting("world.clip", "off"), or simply
   not install fpworld) and take the fill from render::current_fill() instead of the list.
 - app/gfx_rt.h, gfx_rt.cpp: gfxrt::object_walk, a pointer the hook of world_drawObjects hands
   the walk to when it is set (fpworld sets it); null: the code it was.
 - app/app_main.cpp: --world KEY=VALUE, the script / control-socket command "app world info |
   set KEY VALUE", fpworld::install() after the look controls' call-out is registered.
   Not done: world.* lines in the settings file (settings.h keeps only view.* lines).
 - Default is world = views: with the overhead view selected fpworld's call-out is never
   reached and its three hooks are the plain call of the original (one counter each).
